# Cloudflare Pages headers for the node page. Copied verbatim into dist/ by Vite (it is a public/ file).
#
# Everything this build emits is named after its own contents -- /assets/ by Vite, and /porw.<id>/, /vendor.<id>/
# and node_worker.<id>.js by build/runtime.mjs -- so every one of them is immutable and none of them can go stale:
# a new build asks for a different URL.
#
# This file used to ask for five minutes of freshness on the fixed paths /porw/*, /vendor/* and /node_worker.js
# instead, which is a weaker thing and, on the deployed domain, was not even granted. Measured 2026-09-20: those
# paths came back `max-age=14400` on fly.ai.gg against `max-age=300` on *.pages.dev, because the zone's Browser
# Cache TTL raises any shorter origin max-age. /assets/* was untouched -- it asks for longer. So the rule that was
# protecting the files that change was the one being overridden, and the consequence was seen in a real tab
# minutes after a deploy: the new bundle called an op on a four-hour-old cached worker that had never heard of it,
# and the page hung with no error at all. A cache header is a request; a content-addressed URL is not.
#
# Deliberately absent: COOP/COEP. The node runs the single-memory kernel in a plain worker and never touches
# SharedArrayBuffer, and require-corp would force every cross-origin fetch -- including a 28 MB brain from the
# mirror or a Greenfield SP -- to be CORS-clean or carry CORP. Also absent: a connect-src CSP, because the
# relayer URL and the brain URL are both typed in by whoever is running the node.

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/porw.*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/vendor.*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/node_worker.*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
